#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121083 :tutorial/content "If a surface is parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ then the **fundamental vector product** of the surface is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v = \n\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂u} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn particular, if the fundamental vector product of a surface is nonzero at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ then it defines a normal vector to the surface at this point.\n\nLet's find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle \\sqrt{2}\\cos{u}, \\: \\sqrt{2}\\sin{u}, \\: v \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1b86df9-267c-5991-bc04-39d7b75cdf3a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121084 :tutorial/content "Let $S$ be a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane. If each point on $S$ corresponds to exactly one point in $D,$ then we can calculate the surface area of $S$ as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nFor example, let's calculate the surface area of the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\:u-v, \\: v \\big\\rangle,\n$$\n\nthat corresponds to $0 \\leq u \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}=⟨2u,\\,1,\\,0⟩,\\,{r}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,-2u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,-2u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2u)^{2}+(-2u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f5dbe73-8e80-5854-b25a-e502b5f76869" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5335 :tutorial/title "Surface Areas of Parametric Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121095 :tutorial/content "Let $S$ be a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane. We wish to show that the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's consider a partition $P_1 \\times P_2$ of the domain $D,$ where \n\n$$\nP_1 = \\left\\{u_0,u_1,\\ldots, u_m\\right\\},\\qquad P_2 = \\left\\{v_0,v_1,\\ldots, v_n\\right\\},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta u_i = u_{i} - u_{i-1}, \\qquad \\Delta v_j = v_{j} - v_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nThis partition generates a curved grid on the surface, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d31bced1b87e460ec115f97b69a51d21261184b52011e26f32a00487e7cd2d1.png)\n\nNow consider a \"patch\" $S_{ij}$ of the surface that corresponds to the rectangle $R_{ij}$ of the partition. How can we approximate the area of this small patch?\n\nFirst, let's make a bigger picture of $S_{ij}.$\n\n![](images/a1/a125124ac911c9e0557760d9f29ed7ae829f6595be58fec4f2ba39989e51f51b.png)\n\nHere, we assume that the grid curves ${\\color{blue}C_u}$ with equation $\\mathbf{r}(u,v_j)$ and ${\\color{red}C_v}$ with equation $\\mathbf{r}(u_i,v)$ pass through the point $P_{ij}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{r}(u_i,v_j).$\n\nWe now proceed to find a parallelogram whose area is approximately equal to the area of our patch.\n\n- The vector that connects the bottom-left and bottom-right vertices of our patch is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u_{i+1}, v_j) - \\mathbf r (u_i, v_j).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated using a partial derivative, this vector is approximately equal to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_u(u_i,v_j) \\Delta{u}_i,\n$$\n\nwhich is the vector that spans the bottom edge of the parallelogram shown in the diagram.\n\n- Similarly, the vector that connects the bottom-left and top-left vertices of our patch is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u_{i}, v_{j+1}) - \\mathbf r (u_i, v_j).\n$$\n\nThis vector is approximately equal to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_v(u_i,v_j) \\Delta{v}_j,\n$$\n\nwhich is the vector that spans the left edge of the parallelogram shown in the diagram.\n\nAs a result, we can approximate the area of $S_{ij}$ by the area of the parallelogram spanned by these two vectors. The area of such a parallelogram equals the magnitude of the corresponding cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({S}_{ij}) & \\approx ‖{r}_{u}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})Δ{u}_{i}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})Δ{v}_{j}‖ \\\\  & =‖{r}_{u}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})\\times {r}_{v}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})‖\\,Δ{u}_{i}Δ{v}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the approximations for all patches, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(S) & \\approx {\\sum }_{i=0}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}A({S}_{ij}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=0}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}‖{r}_{u}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})\\times {r}_{v}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})‖\\,Δ{u}_{i}Δ{v}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Riemann sum, and the closer the points $P_{ij}$ are to each other, the better our approximation will be. So, in the limit as $m,n\\to\\infty$ (or $\\Delta u_i, \\Delta v_j \\to 0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b0dbabd-ffbc-599c-9653-0ee464f1e4d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5509 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"}]}
